.TH TIMBERSH 1 "2026-08-28" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timbersh \- an interactive shell for timberfs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timbersh
.RI [ --cmd " ARGV" ]
.RI [ --hosts " H,H" ]
.RI [ --rc " FILE" ]
.RI [ --ttl " SECS" ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A console for reading timberfs stores, in a grammar borrowed from SQL. It
selects the stores to read with a predicate, searches them, and pages
through the answer.
.PP
⚠
.B EXPERIMENTAL.
The statements it accepts are NOT promised and change without notice. It
exists to be USED \(em a protocol nobody writes a client against is one
whose awkward parts stay theoretical, and every awkward part this has
found became a fix in timberfs. Do not build anything on its grammar.
.PP
It speaks
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
and nothing else: every statement becomes a document handed to a timberfs
on standard input, and every answer is read back the same way. It
implements no part of the store SELECTOR \(em each host resolves its own,
so a predicate means here exactly what it means there.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " ARGV"
How to reach a timberfs; it is handed the document on stdin. Default
.BR "timberfs query --query -" .
Anything that reaches one works \(em a wrapper,
.BR ssh (1),
a container exec.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_CMD )
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " H,H"
Fan out to these hosts, substituting each for
.B _TIMBERHOST_
in
.BR \-\-cmd .
Their stores present as one set with a
.B HOST
column, each remembering where it lives.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_HOSTS )
.RS
.PP
A read goes to every host at once and is rendered in the order they were
given. There is no order claimed BETWEEN hosts, for the reason a bounded
timberfs answer is
.B order=sequential
(see
.BR timberfs\-records (5)).
A
.B limit
is a total, so a host whose output falls past it did work that is never
shown \(em the price of not paying the latency serially.
.PP
Several hosts with no
.B _TIMBERHOST_
in
.B \-\-cmd
is refused: every host would get the same command, so one forest would be
read N times and every entry returned N times under a different host's
name.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-rc " FILE"
Statements run at startup, default
.IR ~/.timbershrc .
It is RUN, not read \(em a script of statements, the way
.I .psqlrc
is.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_RC )
.TP
.BI \-\-ttl " SECS"
Expire the cached store list after SECS. Default 0, which never expires
it \(em
.B refresh
re-reads it and says what changed.
.RB ( $TIMBERSH_STORE_TTL )
.TP
.B \-q
Start without printing the help.
.SH STATEMENTS
.B \(rsd
lists the stores;
.B \(rsd NAME
or
.B \(rsd ID
describes one.
.B show hosts;
says what each host is and whether anything it said went wrong.
.B \(rs?
prints the grammar, which is the authority \(em this page deliberately
does not copy it, because it moves.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
